The conserved ratio $Y_B$ is defined as the ratio of the net baryon number density, $(n_B - n_{ ar{B}})$, to the entropy density, $s$. Here, $n_B$ represents the number density of baryons (particles composed of three quarks, like protons and neutrons), and $n_{ ar{B}}$ represents the number density of antibaryons (antiparticles of baryons). The entropy density $s$ measures the disorder or the number of accessible microstates in the system. The term $Y_B =  rac{n_B - n_{ ar{B}}}{s}$ is approximately constant in relativistic heavy-ion collisions and in the early universe, provided that baryon number violation is negligible. The approximation $Y_B 
